To understand how real databases work, I built KvStore, a key-value database engine in C# (.NET 10), starting with limited knowledge of .NET internals and networking. I developed it in phases: an in-memory store, file persistence, a checksummed write-ahead log with crash recovery, an LSM-tree with flush and compaction, and an ASP.NET Core HTTP API. I tested crash recovery in two ways: unit tests that damage the files on purpose, and a test that kills the writing process in the middle of a write ten times per run and checks that no saved record is lost. Benchmarks on my laptop showed the price of durability: about 1,000 writes per second when every write is forced to disk, against roughly 12,000 to 31,000 when it is not. The project taught me file formats, testing failure, concurrency and measuring before concluding, and its README lists its limits honestly, such as a single global lock and no replication. [Optional: I built it step by step with an AI assistant's guidance, tested every phase myself, and can explain each part.]
